On Saturday, July 27, 2013 12:20:42 AM UTC+4, stevo wrote:
>
> Of course, "umami" can also be an adjective in English. All you have to do 
> is use it that way. 
>

Unfortunately not all people know what is umami. So I added this awkward 
wording "has umami taste" to explicitly show what I'm talking about.
When more people learn about this word the *English* definition might be 
changed (without changing lojbani semantics).

E.g. recently in bpfk-list I suggested changing the definition of {gleki} 
from
x1 is happy/merry/gay/glad/gleeful about x2 (event/state).
to 
x1 is happy/merry/glad/gleeful about x2 (event/state).

just because one English word completely changed it's meaning.



> stevo
